      'SEBoK places Business or Mission Analysis first in Concept Definition. Its purpose is to understand a mission or market problem, threat or opportunity and to establish the goals, objectives and measures of success of a potential solution class. The solution need not be a new system: it could be a change to an existing system, a service, or an operational change. Success depends on the team understanding the purpose of the system of interest (why), what would make a solution acceptable or desirable (what), the measures (how well), and the operating environment (in what conditions). Business or Mission Analysis is often performed iteratively with Stakeholder Needs Definition.',
      'The problem, threat or opportunity is defined in four steps: identify the strategic and business-operations stakeholders who are affected or would benefit; work with them to understand how; state the problem clearly; and obtain their concurrence with the statement. From that statement come the MGOs. The mission states why the project exists, why the system is needed and what value it brings. Goals are elaborated from the mission and divide it into manageable pieces that the team and the business stakeholders understand in the same way. Objectives are elaborated from the goals and say in more detail what must be done for the goals and the mission to be achieved. Measures are quantitative metrics, expressed as measures of effectiveness (MOE), used to validate the system against the objectives and to manage development across the life cycle. These are four levels, not three: in SEBoK’s own example the consumer objective is qualitative (“receive finished home brew coffee within minutes upon request”) and the number sits in a separate consumer-focused measure (“finished home brew provided within 2 minutes of activation”); the business objective “increase market share” is likewise paired with the measure “improve current market share by 20 %” (R10 p.546).',
